  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DRA Gaza ACJR 2025</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">AAP: Plan is a Humanitarian Accountability Partnership (HAP) certified agency. Plan International ensured significant involvement of communities. Project participants will continue to be consulted by Taawon and Plan, and complaint/feedback mechanisms will be available and distributed widely and clearly. Plan also has a toll-free line for Complaints and Feedback which will be communicated to beneficiaries. 

Conflict-sensitivity:  Plan International is a CHS verified organisation, and Taawon has trained staff in IHL, safeguarding, and PSHEA . Through the due-diligence process, plan International Jordan checked Taawon&apos;s policies and they adhere to Plan International guidelines to safeguarding, gender sensitivity, and PSHEA.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SUMMARY
Plan International Netherlands jointly with the Dutch Relief Alliance (DRA), and in response to the ceasefire agreement in Gaza (OPT) in the Middle East, have launched an Acute Crisis Joint Response (ACJR) to address the acute humanitarian needs of internally displaced persons (IDPs) in the Gaza Strip. It aims to provide most vulnerable families with food parcels, hygiene kits, and dignity kits, ensuring their basic needs are met for one month. Additionally, the project will deliver crucial mental health and psychosocial support (MHPSS) interventions for children affected by the conflict, addressing the rising cases of child malnutrition and psychological distress.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Gaza Emergency Relief program addresses the acute humanitarian needs of internally displaced persons (IDPs) in the Gaza Strip. Recent assessments indicate that approximately 1.9 million people&#x2014;90% of the population&#x2014;have been displaced due to ongoing hostilities. IDP families, particularly in the Middle and Southern regions, are enduring severe shortages of food, hygiene supplies, and essential mental health services. 345,000 people are facing catastrophic food insecurity (IPC) and more than 1 million children are in need of MHPSS (UNICEF). The WASH Cluster notes scarcity and unaffordability of basic hygiene products, including menstrual hygiene items, have compromised personal hygiene and dignity. 
In response to these critical gaps, the program aims to provide 1,250 families with food parcels, hygiene kits, and dignity kits, ensuring their basic needs are met for one month. Additionally, the project will deliver crucial mental health and psychosocial support (MHPSS) interventions for children affected by the conflict, addressing the rising cases of child malnutrition and psychological distress.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The intervention logic addresses urgent and multi-faceted needs of IDPs in the Middle and South areas of Gaza as identified in the RNAs: FSL, WASH and Protection, through a targeted and evidence-based approach. The program, designed and implemented in partnership with Taawon Welfare Association, focuses on provision of food parcels, hygiene kits, dignity kits, and psychosocial support (MHPSS) to provide life-saving support to the most impacted population. 

The intervention responds to the findings of recent assessments that highlight severe shortages in food, hygiene supplies, and mental health services. The planned activities are interlinked to provide a holistic response for 1,250 families (7,450 individuals): food assistance will help alleviate hunger and malnutrition, while hygiene and dignity kits will support safe and healthy living conditions, particularly for women and girls. Meanwhile, MHPSS services will address the severe psychological distress faced by children (1,832 individuals), enhancing their coping mechanisms and resilience.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will prioritize vulnerable groups impacted by the crisis including female and child headed households, separated and unaccompanied children, women, children and adolescents with special needs, women, children and adolescents at risk of abuse, exploitation and GBV, IDPs, and host communities.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will use partnership-based delivery, leveraging Taawon&apos;s expertise and local presence for efficient and contextually appropriate aid distribution. Taawon participates in cluster meetings and works closely with communities. Project design is based on their needs assessments and community consultation. A community-centred approach prioritises the most vulnerable IDPs, particularly women, children, and individuals with disabilities. 

Beneficiary identification will be conducted with stakeholders to ensure the project responds to the needs of most vulnerable population. Procurement will follow established policies to ensure quality, timely delivery. Taawon&apos;s market analysis has identified vendors in line with Taawon and Plan&apos;s policies and procedures. Distribution will take place through 6 weeks at IDP shelters and host communities, with trained staff ensuring an orderly, dignified, and transparent process. 

Plan has a partnership agreement with Taawon, and has implemented life-saving projects in Gaza. Plan will monitor implementation via reports, photos, beneficiaries feedback and financial monitoring. Robust monitoring and accountability mechanisms include beneficiary feedback and cluster coordination. Regular reporting and documentation will ensure transparency and compliance with humanitarian standards, enhancing efficiency and impact. Coordination with JR members and partners has and will take place via bilateral meetings and working group participation.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will use partnership-based delivery, leveraging Taawon&apos;s expertise and local presence for efficient and contextually appropriate aid distribution. Taawon participates in cluster meetings and works closely with communities. Project design is based on their needs assessments and community consultation. A community-centred approach prioritises the most vulnerable IDPs, particularly women, children, and individuals with disabilities. 

Beneficiary identification will be conducted with stakeholders to ensure the project responds to the needs of most vulnerable population. Procurement will follow established policies to ensure quality, timely delivery. Taawon&apos;s market analysis has identified vendors in line with Taawon and Plan&apos;s policies and procedures. Distribution will take place through 6 weeks at IDP shelters and host communities, with trained staff ensuring an orderly, dignified, and transparent process. 

Plan has a partnership agreement with Taawon, and has implemented life-saving projects in Gaza. Plan will monitor implementation via reports, photos, beneficiaries feedback and financial monitoring. Robust monitoring and accountability mechanisms include beneficiary feedback and cluster coordination. Regular reporting and documentation will ensure transparency and compliance with humanitarian standards, enhancing efficiency and impact. Coordination with JR members and partners has and will take place via bilateral meetings and working group participation.
The project will be implemented over 6 weeks. Beneficiaries are already selected, and Taawon has a framework agreement with vendors, ensuring proper coordination with local stakeholders and alignment with humanitarian standards. The first week will be dedicated to signing the partnership agreement with the local partners based on the proposal, and will include the identification of the children who will participate in the MHPSS sessions.  In weeks two to four, the distribution of food parcels, hygiene kits, and dignity kits will take place alongside the implementation of counselling sessions and MHPSS interventions for children. The fifth week will focus on final distributions and addressing any remaining needs.

The final week will be dedicated to evaluation and reporting, including data collection, beneficiary feedback, and lessons learned to inform future interventions. Continuous monitoring will be conducted throughout the project to ensure timely and effective implementation.</narrative>
  </description>
